WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
Memory ownership across ABI boundaries is a common source of defects.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:14.920
If one component allocates memory and another frees it with a different allocator, runtime, build mode, or cleanup convention, behavior can become unsafe.

3
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:23.500
This is especially important across DLL boundaries, plugins, custom allocators, third-party libraries, and mixed debug or release builds.

4
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.080
The problem is not just allocation.

5
00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:29.060
It is the entire ownership lifecycle.

6
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:37.420
Defensive APIs define who owns each object, which function releases it, and whether the caller may copy, retain, mutate, or share it.

7
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.760
A returned pointer without a release rule is not a complete contract.

8
00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:47.940
Passing containers or strings across a boundary can also carry allocator and layout assumptions.

9
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:55.000
Even when the code compiles, the binary contract may assume a runtime pairing the deployment does not actually provide.

10
00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:03.120
Release functions, custom deleters, opaque handles, and RAII wrappers can make cleanup rules explicit and reviewable.

11
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:07.140
A library that creates an object can provide the matching destroy function.

12
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:10.520
A caller-side wrapper can then call that function automatically.

13
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.300
This keeps ownership visible while avoiding mismatched cleanup.

14
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:22.560
Review should ask what happens on normal release, error paths, early shutdown, repeated cleanup, and version mismatch.

15
00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:28.260
Tests should include the exact allocation and release paths that cross the boundary.

16
00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:33.580
Ownership rules should be written in a way a caller can follow without reading implementation code.

17
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:43.840
State whether returned memory is borrowed or owned, whether buffers can be retained after the call, whether the caller may mutate data, and which function performs cleanup.

18
00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:49.300
The safer API is usually the one where the release path is boring, obvious, and hard to get wrong.
